{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.11.11","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceId":105874,"databundleVersionId":12964783,"sourceType":"competition"}],"dockerImageVersionId":31040,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Fake or Real: The Impostor Hunt in Texts\n\nApproach with **Pairwise Ranking Prompting (PRP)**, which is a technique where you prompt a large language model (LLM) to compare two texts and decide which one better matches a given criterion (e.g., which is more \"real,\" more relevant, or preferred).\n\n**Pairwise prompt:**\n```\nprompt = f\"\"\"Which of the following two passages is more real?\n\nPassage A:\n{text1}\n\nPassage B:\n{text2}\n\nAnswer with \"Passage A\" or \"Passage B\" only.\"\"\"\nresponse = llm(prompt)[0]['generated_text']\n```\n\n**PRP unit:**\n![image.png](attachment:0199abe2-fd85-4103-a9fa-7363bf518a84.png)\nJian Luo, Xuanang Chen, Ben He, and Le Sun. 2024. PRP-Graph: Pairwise Ranking Prompting to LLMs with Graph Aggregation for Effective Text Re-ranking. In _Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)_, pages 5766–5776, Bangkok, Thailand. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\nI will also use **prompt engineering** by including representative examples from the training data directly in the prompt (few-shot learning). For pairwise ranking, this means showing the model several example pairs and the correct choice before presenting the actual pair to be ranked.\n","metadata":{},"attachments":{"0199abe2-fd85-4103-a9fa-7363bf518a84.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Imports","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":true}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#pip install free-llm-toolbox","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true,"_kg_hide-output":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import os\nos.environ['TF_CPP_MIN_LOG_LEVEL'] = '3'  # Suppress TensorFlow logs\nimport warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\nimport random\nimport string\nfrom tqdm import tqdm\nfrom IPython.display import clear_output\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport plotly.express as px\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nclear_output()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true,"_kg_hide-output":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## About the Data\nThe dataset is derived from a diverse collection of articles originally published in [The Messenger](https://messenger.eso.org/) journals, with each article pair containing a \"Real\" and a \"Fake\" version that have both been extensively modified using large language models (LLMs) and various preprocessing techniques. \n\nThe data are organized into separate `train` and `test` directories, each containing randomly numbered articles, where every article has two associated text files (`file_1.txt` and `file_2.txt`); one file contains the Real version and the other the Fake version, with their order randomized. [More details here...](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/fake-or-real-the-impostor-hunt/data)","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"class Config:\n    PROJECT_NAME = \"Text Imposter Hunt - European Space Agency\"\n    BASE_PATH = \"/kaggle/input/fake-or-real-the-impostor-hunt/data/\"\n    TRAIN_PATH = os.path.join(BASE_PATH, \"train\")\n    TEST_PATH = os.path.join(BASE_PATH, \"test\")\n    TRAIN_GT_PATH = os.path.join(BASE_PATH, \"train.csv\")\n    SUBMISSION_FILE = \"submission.csv\"\n    RANDOM_STATE = 42\n    SEQUENCE_LENGTH = 128\n    LLM = None\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def set_seeds(seed=42):\n        os.environ['PYTHONHASHSEED'] = str(seed)          # For Python hash-based operations\n        random.seed(seed)                                 # For Python's built-in random module\n        np.random.seed(seed)                              # For NumPy\n\nCFG = Config()\nCFG.set_seeds(CFG.RANDOM_STATE)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true,"_kg_hide-output":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def read_texts_from_dir(dir_path, debug=False):\n    \"\"\"\n    Reads 'file_1.txt' and 'file_2.txt' from each subfolder in the directory\n    and returns a DataFrame with columns: id, file_1, file_2\n\n    Args:\n        dir_path (str): Path to the parent directory containing subfolders.\n        debug (bool): If True, raises errors immediately for debugging.\n\n    Returns:\n        pd.DataFrame: DataFrame with columns ['id', 'file_1', 'file_2']\n    \"\"\"\n    data = []\n    error_count = 0\n    last_error = None\n\n    for folder_name in tqdm(sorted(os.listdir(dir_path))):\n        folder_path = os.path.join(dir_path, folder_name)\n        file1_path = os.path.join(folder_path, 'file_1.txt')\n        file2_path = os.path.join(folder_path, 'file_2.txt')\n\n        try:\n            with open(file1_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f1:\n                text1 = f1.read().strip()\n            with open(file2_path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f2:\n                text2 = f2.read().strip()\n\n            # Extract numeric id from folder name\n            index = int(folder_name.split('_')[-1])\n            data.append((index, text1, text2))\n\n        except (FileNotFoundError, ValueError, OSError) as e:\n            error_count += 1\n            last_error = e\n            if debug:\n                raise e\n\n    print(f\"Read {len(data)} records with {error_count} errors\")\n    if error_count > 0:\n        print(\"Last Error:\", last_error)\n\n    return pd.DataFrame(data, columns=['id', 'file_1', 'file_2'])\n\ntrain = read_texts_from_dir(CFG.TRAIN_PATH)\ntrain_gt = pd.read_csv(CFG.TRAIN_GT_PATH)\ntest = read_texts_from_dir(CFG.TEST_PATH)\ntrain = train.merge(train_gt, how='inner', on='id')\ntrain.set_index(\"id\", inplace=True)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-output":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## EDA\n- `Train` has 95 articles (each article has two texts, 1 real 1 fake).\n- `Test` has 1068 articles.\n- Median length is 1373 and 1852 for real and fake texts.\n\nSince the median text lengths far exceed the `sequence_length` of typical models, hence will need account for significant truncation or use models that support longer sequences.\n\n### Missingness\nArticles 10 and 14 in the training set and article 223 in the test set have empty fake text files. I kept these rows, since empty text may appear during prediction.\n\n### Special Characters\nThere are special characters (☉, ি, േ, '്, ...) exclusively in fake train texts (22 out of 95) and never in real train texts suggests these characters are a strong indicator of fake content.\n\n### Article similarity\nSince the distributions of Jaccard similarity, TF-IDF cosine similarity, and semantic similarity are all right-skewed, it indicates that most article pairs have **low similarity scores**—meaning they share little vocabulary, word importance, or contextual meaning. This suggests that the pairs are largely unrelated to each other, rather than being paraphrases or closely matched texts.","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-07-07T02:24:36.083372Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-07-07T02:24:36.083701Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-07-07T02:24:36.096666Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-07-07T02:24:36.083675Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-07-07T02:24:36.095406Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"train_df = train.copy()\ntest_df = test.copy()\n\n# Extract real and fake texts\nreal_texts = train_df.apply(lambda row: row[f'file_{row.real_text_id}'], axis=1)\nfake_texts = train_df.apply(lambda row: row[f'file_{1 if row.real_text_id == 2 else 2}'], axis=1)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true,"_kg_hide-output":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def plot_average_text_length(text_series, title='Distribution of Text Lengths'):\n    lengths = text_series.apply(len)\n    mean_length = lengths.mean()\n    median_length = lengths.median()\n    \n    plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3))\n    plt.hist(lengths, bins=30, color='skyblue', edgecolor='black')\n    plt.axvline(mean_length, color='red', linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2, label=f'Mean: {mean_length:.2f}')\n    plt.axvline(median_length, color='green', linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2, label=f'Median: {median_length:.2f}')\n    plt.title(title)\n    plt.xlabel('Text Length')\n    plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n    plt.legend()\n    plt.show()\n    \n    return mean_length, median_length\n\nplot_average_text_length(real_texts, title=\"Distribution of Real Text Lengths\")\nplot_average_text_length(fake_texts, title=\"Distribution of Fake Text Lengths\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def find_empty_text_rows(df, cols=['file_1', 'file_2']):\n    mask = df[cols].isnull() | (df[cols].apply(lambda x: x.str.strip() == ''))\n    return df[mask.any(axis=1)]\nprint(\"Articles with empty text files in train:\")\nfind_empty_text_rows(train_df)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"Articles with empty text files in test:\")\nfind_empty_text_rows(test_df)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fake_flags = ['☉', 'ি', 'േ', '്', '្', 'ា', 'ំ', '�', 'া', 'ৈ', 'ा', 'ే', 'ు', '۔', 'ַ', '्', 'ి', '！', '」', 'ા', 'ી',\n              'ે', 'ျ', '္', 'ာ', 'ി', '\\u200b', '்', '්', 'ா', '್', 'ು', '😰', 'ീ', 'ّ', 'ो', 'ु', 'ँ', 'ै', 'ि', 'ಾ',\n              'े', 'ิ', 'ে', 'ਾ', 'ု', 'ാ', 'િ', '॰', 'ಿ', '্', 'ુ', '∀', '：', 'ើ', 'ื', '่', 'ு', 'ී', 'ึ', 'ూ',\n              '\\u0ab1', 'ా', '\\u200c', 'ং', 'ೈ', 'ੋ', 'ौ', '€', '。', 'ေ', '◸', 'ू', 'ं', 'ே', '\\u05f6', 'ි',\n              '\\u0ba5', 'ี', '్', '์', '÷', 'ெ', 'ी', '҆', '˚', '̈', '।', '🥶', '\\u05cd', 'ొ', '【', '》', '้', '←', 'ู',\n              'ো', '્', 'ி', 'ో', '਼', 'ો', '≥', '،', 'ു', 'ം', '，', '້', '⠀', '】', '་', 'ុ', '̮', '🎦', 'ీ', 'ૂ',\n              'ં', '）', '़', 'ੇ', '¡', '、', 'ឹ', 'ැ', 'ೆ', 'ุ', 'ু', '？', 'ั', 'ె', 'ୁ', 'ോ', 'ೀ', '／', 'ோ',\n              'ូ', 'ා', '´', '❔', '็', 'ൃ', '՛', '🕊', '≤', 'ൂ', 'ָ', '়', 'ऀ', 'ಂ', 'ਿ', 'ٔ', '；', '့', '်', 'ោ',\n              'ះ', 'ِ', 'ீ', '≕', '（', '㎈', '๋', 'ൈ', 'ဲ', '\\xad', '॒', 'ៃ', 'ৃ', 'َ', '\\x04', '［', 'ೇ']\nprint(f\"Fake Flagged Special Characters ==> {fake_flags[:30]}...\")\n\ndef contains_special_char(text):\n    for ch in text:\n        if ch in fake_flags:\n            return True\n    return False\n\ndef count_special_texts(texts):\n    return sum(contains_special_char(text) for text in texts)\n\nreal_count = count_special_texts(real_texts)\nfake_count = count_special_texts(fake_texts)\nprint(f\"Real Train texts with special chars: {real_count} / {len(real_texts)}\")\nprint(f\"Fake Train texts with special chars: {fake_count} / {len(fake_texts)}\")\n\ntest_df['special_char_flag'] = test_df.apply(lambda x: contains_special_char(x['file_1'])+\n                                contains_special_char(x['file_2']), axis=1)\nprint(f\"Test records where no file has flag chars: {test_df['special_char_flag'].value_counts()[0]}\")\nprint(f\"Test records where one file has flag chars: {test_df['special_char_flag'].value_counts()[1]}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def preprocess(text):\n    tokens = text.lower().split()\n    tokens = [word.strip(string.punctuation) for word in tokens]\n    return [word for word in tokens if word]\n\ndef apply_similarity(df, similarity_func):\n    return df.apply(lambda x: similarity_func(x['file_1'], x['file_2']), axis=1)\n\ndef plot_similarity(df, col, title, xlabel):\n    plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3))\n    plt.hist(df[col], bins=20, color='skyblue', edgecolor='black')\n    plt.title(title)\n    plt.xlabel(xlabel)\n    plt.ylabel('Number of Article Pairs')\n    plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Jaccard Similarity\nJaccard similarity measures how much two sets overlap, and in text analysis, it’s often used to compare the unique words in two documents.\n$$ \\text{Jaccard similarity} = \\frac{A\\cap B}{A\\cup B}$$\nWhere A and B are the sets of unique words from each text.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nRange: The value ranges from 0 to 1.\n- 0 means the two texts share no words in common.\n- 1 means the two texts have exactly the same set of unique words.\n  \nCloser to 1: Higher overlap in vocabulary; the texts are more similar in terms of the words they use. \\\nCloser to 0: Little or no overlap; the texts are likely unrelated or discuss very different topics.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def jaccard_similarity(text1, text2):\n    set1 = set(preprocess(text1))\n    set2 = set(preprocess(text2))\n    intersection = set1.intersection(set2)\n    union = set1.union(set2)\n    return len(intersection) / len(union) if union else 0.0\n\ntrain_df['jaccard_similarity'] = apply_similarity(train_df, jaccard_similarity)\ntest_df['jaccard_similarity'] = apply_similarity(test_df, jaccard_similarity)\n\nplot_similarity(train_df, 'jaccard_similarity', 'Jaccard Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Train Data')\nplot_similarity(test_df, 'jaccard_similarity', 'Jaccard Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Test Data')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### TF-IDF + Cosine Similarity\n\nTF-IDF + cosine similarity measures how similar two documents are by comparing the importance of each word (using TF-IDF weighting) and calculating the cosine of the angle between their vector representations.\n$$ \\text{Cosine similarity} = \\frac{A \\cdot B}{||A||\\text{ }||B||}$$\nwhere A and B are the TF-IDF vectors of the two documents.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nRange: The value ranges from 0 to 1.\n- 0 means the documents have no similarity in their weighted word usage.\n- 1 means the documents are identical in their TF-IDF representation (i.e., they use the same words with the same importance).\n  \nCloser to 1: The documents share similar vocabulary and word importance, indicating high relatedness in content. \\\nCloser to 0: The documents are dissimilar in both word choice and importance, suggesting they discuss different topics or use different language.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def tfidf_cosine_similarity(text1, text2):\n    vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words='english')\n    tfidf_matrix = vectorizer.fit_transform([text1, text2])\n    return cosine_similarity(tfidf_matrix[0:1], tfidf_matrix[1:2])[0][0]\n\ntrain_df['tfidf_cosine_similarity'] = apply_similarity(train_df, tfidf_cosine_similarity)\ntest_df['tfidf_cosine_similarity'] = apply_similarity(test_df, tfidf_cosine_similarity)\n\nplot_similarity(train_df, 'tfidf_cosine_similarity', 'TF-IDF + Cosine Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Train Data')\nplot_similarity(test_df, 'tfidf_cosine_similarity', 'TF-IDF + Cosine Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Test Data')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Semantic similarity\n\nSemantic similarity measures how closely two documents are related in meaning, regardless of their exact wording. I used neural language models (such as BERT or Sentence Transformers) to generate dense vector representations (embeddings) that capture the contextual meaning of entire texts.\n$$ \\text{Cosine similarity} = \\frac{A \\cdot B}{||A||\\text{ }||B||}$$\nwhere A and B are the TF-IDF vectors of the two documents.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nRange: The value ranges from 0 to 1.\n- 0 means the documents are semantically unrelated.\n- 1 means the documents are identical in their contextual meaning.\n  \nCloser to 1:  Indicates the documents are highly similar in meaning, even if they use different words or phrasing. \\\nCloser to 0: Suggests the documents discuss different topics, ideas, or contexts.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def semantic_similarity(text1, text2):\n    emb1 = model.encode(text1, convert_to_tensor=True)\n    emb2 = model.encode(text2, convert_to_tensor=True)\n    score = util.pytorch_cos_sim(emb1, emb2).item()\n    return score\n\ntrain_df['semantic_similarity'] = apply_similarity(train_df, tfidf_cosine_similarity)\ntest_df['semantic_similarity'] = apply_similarity(test_df, tfidf_cosine_similarity)\n\nplot_similarity(train_df, 'semantic_similarity', 'Semantic Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Train Data')\nplot_similarity(test_df, 'semantic_similarity', 'Semantic Similarity', 'Distribution of Similarity in Test Data')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Preprocessing\nThe preprocessing function standardizes raw text inputs for transformer models by applying tokenization, truncation, and padding. Each input string is converted into a sequence of token IDs using a pretrained tokenizer, with all sequences adjusted to a fixed length (`max_length`). This process also generates attention masks to distinguish real tokens from padding, ensuring that the model focuses on meaningful content. The output is a dictionary of PyTorch tensors (`input_ids`, `attention_mask`, and, if applicable, `token_type_ids`).","metadata":{"_kg_hide-input":false}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Modelling\n\n### Evaluation Metric\nSince the evaluation for this project is based on pairwise accuracy, which measures how effectively models can align with human preferences by selecting the preferred text within each given pair. This means that, rather than simply classifying individual texts as \"real\" or \"fake,\" the modeling approach must shift to a comparative framework. \n\n\nThere are several modeling options that are well-suited for pairwise text comparison tasks:\n\n1. Transformer-Based Pairwise Models\n\nFine-tuned Transformers (BERT, RoBERTa, DeBERTa):\nThese models take both texts in a pair as input (formatted as [CLS] text_1 [SEP] text_2 [SEP]) and are highly effective at capturing deep semantic relationships, making them state-of-the-art for text-pair classification and ranking.\n\nSentence Transformers:\nThese generate embeddings for each text and enable direct comparison through similarity scoring, which is particularly valuable for semantic similarity and paraphrase detection.\n\n2. Pairwise Ranking with Large Language Models\n\nPairwise Ranking Prompting (PRP) with LLMs:\nPrompting large language models like Flan-UL2 or GPT-4 to choose between two texts has shown impressive results for preference alignment and ranking tasks, especially when fine-tuned or used with well-crafted prompts.\n\n3. Siamese and Twin Network Architectures\n\nSiamese Networks:|\nThese architectures encode each text with identical subnetworks and then compare their outputs, which is especially useful when the relationship between texts is more important than their independent classification.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Pairwise Ranking Prompting (PRP)\nThis is a technique where you prompt a large language model (LLM) to compare two texts and decide which one better matches a given criterion (e.g., which is more \"real,\" more relevant, or preferred)\n\n```\nprompt = f\"\"\"Which of the following two passages is more real?\n\nPassage A:\n{text1}\n\nPassage B:\n{text2}\n\nAnswer with \"Passage A\" or \"Passage B\" only.\"\"\"\nresponse = llm(prompt)[0]['generated_text']\n```\n\nTo leverage the training data, we can consider the following techniques:\n1. **Fine-tuning**: futher train the LLM on the labelled training data and adjusts internal weights. Though we don't have aubstantial amount of labeled pairs (only 95).\n2. **RAG**: at inference time, retrieve relevant examples or facts from your training data and provide them as additional context to the LLM.\n3. **Prompt Engineering**: Include representative examples from your training data directly in the prompt (few-shot learning). For pairwise ranking, this means showing the model several example pairs and the correct choice before presenting the actual pair to be ranked.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Prompt Engineering\n\nGiven the constraints of large language model input windows and the volume of article pairs, it is necessary to employ **chain prompting** in conjunction with text **truncation** and **segmentation** strategies. Chain prompting enables the decomposition of the overall comparison task into sequential, manageable sub-tasks, facilitating stepwise analysis and iterative refinement of the model’s outputs. By splitting lengthy articles into smaller, context-preserving chunks, the model can process each segment within its token limitations while maintaining coherence across the full text.","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-07-10T21:14:49.206555Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-07-10T21:14:49.206911Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-07-10T21:14:49.210830Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-07-10T21:14:49.206884Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-07-10T21:14:49.209920Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"train_df = train.copy()\ntest_df = test.copy()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def chunk_text(text, max_tokens):\n    words = text.split()\n    return [' '.join(words[i:i+max_tokens]) for i in range(0, len(words), max_tokens)]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"_kg_hide-input":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Prompts\ndef initial_prompt(real, fake):\n    prompt = (\n        \"You are given two texts.\\n\"\n        \"Text 1 is real.\\n\"\n        \"Text 2 is fake.\\n\\n\"\n        \"Analyze both text and explore the reasons why Text 1 is authentic and Text 2 is not.\\n\"\n        \"Consider these aspects:\\n\"\n        \"- Consistency: Is the text logically consistent and free of contradictions?\\n\"\n        \"- Language and Style: Does the writing style match what is expected for real content of this type?\\n\"\n        \"- Specific Details: Are there concrete details, data, or context that support authenticity?\\n\"\n        \"- Common Signs of Fabrication: Does the fake text contain generic statements, errors, inconsistencies, or implausible claims?\\n\\n\"\n        \"Provide a concise summary explaining why Text 1 is real and Text 2 is fake.\\n\"\n        f\"Text 1: {real}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"Text 2: {fake_}\\n\"\n    )\n    return prompt\n\ndef refinement_prompt(previous_summary, real_chunk, fake_chunk):\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a refinement prompt for a single chunk pair.\n    \"\"\"\n    prompt = (\n        \"You are given two text.\\n\"\n        \"Text 1 is real.\\n\"\n        \"Text 2 is fake.\\n\\n\"\n        \"Your task is to refine the previous summary explaining why Text 1 is real and Text 2 is fake, \"\n        \"incorporating new analysis from the following texts.\\n\\n\"\n        f\"Previous summary:\\n{previous_summary}\\n\\n\"\n        \"New texts to 